/// The two text operations the generator needs that NOTHING ELSE CARRIES.
///
/// ⚠️ NAMED `Markup` BECAUSE `Strings` COLLIDED WITH THE LIBRARY'S `strings`,
/// and not merely in the reader's eye. `algc --compile` names each emitted C
/// file after its unit, so on a case-insensitive filesystem `strings.c`
/// overwrote `Strings.c` and the build failed with five errors about a
/// function nothing declared. The interpreter is fine with the pair; `algvm`
/// loses the second unit and raises `Undefined variable`. A defect, reported,
/// with a reproduction in DEFECTS-FOUND.md -- and this rename is the way round
/// it rather than the fix.
///
/// ⚠️ THIS UNIT USED TO HOLD SIX. `Trim`, `Replace` and `Split` now come from
/// the library's own `strings`, imported by path -- and `IsSpace` went with
/// them, having existed only to serve the `Trim` that is gone. What is left is
/// what the library does not have: `StartsWith`, and an `Escape` that is about
/// HTML rather than about strings.
///
/// The two were measured against each other before the swap, over 15,000 lines
/// holding em-dashes plus twenty passes of `Replace` on a page-sized string:
/// identical answers, the library 5% slower, and no algorithmic difference --
/// both walk once with `Pos`'s start index. The one behavioural difference is
/// deliberate on the library's side: `Replace (T, '', X)` RAISES there where
/// this unit answered T unchanged, and `Split (T, '')` likewise. Every needle
/// in this generator is a non-empty literal, so the difference cannot arise --
/// and raising is the better of the two answers if it ever could.
///
/// ⚠️ A single pass is still not linear time on long text. A character index
/// into a string holding any non-ASCII character walks from the start to find
/// the byte offset, so the cost of the calls in one pass grows with how far
/// into the text they reach. Measured on the 352 KB specification, which is
/// full of warning signs and em-dashes, `Split` took 3.40s; on ASCII text of
/// the same size, 0.11s. That is a property of the language and not of whose
/// `Split` it is. Read a file with `Template.ReadLines` and work a line at a
/// time, and the question never arises.
unit Markup;

/// ⚠️ Imported BY PATH, which is [MOD-001]: a bare identifier names a file of
/// that name with '.a24' appended, and a quoted string is a path -- resolved
/// beside this file first, so '../vendor/...' reaches the pinned submodule.
/// Nothing has to be copied, symlinked or installed, and the extension is
/// still appended, so the path is written without one.
uses '../vendor/algol24-library/strings';

/// Whether Text begins with Prefix.
///
/// An empty prefix begins everything, the empty string included.
function StartsWith (Text : String, Prefix : String) : Boolean;
var
    N := 0;

begin
    N := Length (Prefix);

    if N > Length (Text) then Exit False;

    Exit Copy (Text, 0, N) = Prefix;
end

/// Text with the three characters HTML reserves replaced by entities.
///
/// The ampersand is replaced first. Doing it last would rewrite the ampersands
/// that the other two had just introduced, and every entity on the page would
/// arrive doubly escaped.
function Escape (Text : String) : String;
var
    S := '';

begin
    S := Replace(Text, '&', '&amp;');
    S := Replace(S, '<', '&lt;');

    Exit Replace(S, '>', '&gt;');
end

test 'StartsWith Answers For A Prefix And A Non Prefix';
begin
    AssertTrue (StartsWith ('## Heading', '##'));
    AssertTrue (not StartsWith ('# Heading', '##'));
end

test 'StartsWith Takes An Empty Prefix And A Prefix Longer Than The Text';
begin
    AssertTrue (StartsWith ('', ''));
    AssertTrue (StartsWith ('abc', ''));
    AssertTrue (not StartsWith ('ab', 'abc'));
end

test 'Escape Replaces The Three Reserved Characters';
begin
    AssertEqual('&lt;pre&gt;', Escape('<pre>'));
    AssertEqual('&amp;amp;', Escape('&amp;'));
end
